@meta
id: 14-10
title: Ask me each time
chapter: 14
type: lesson
skills: sub-confirm
par: 46
minutes: 7
vscode: Ctrl-H, then Replace (not Replace All) eleven times, reading each one
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
The `status` field on orders is becoming `state`. In this provider
that means the parameter, the comparisons, the update values and the
event payload. Whole-word anchors get you most of the way.

They do not get you all of the way. Four hits of the whole word
`status` in this file are not the field: a module path in an import,
the subject line of an email a customer receives, the URL that email
links to, and the name of an event other services subscribe to. Change
any of those and something outside this file breaks, and the type
checker will not tell you.

You could write a pattern clever enough to skip them. Or you could add
one flag that shows you each match and waits for a yes or a no. Use
the flag. This is the habit the chapter is named after: whenever you
cannot see every hit on the screen, ask.

@keys
| `:%s/\<status\>/state/gc` `Enter` | the `c` flag: confirm each match |
| `y` | replace this one |
| `n` | skip this one |
| `a` | replace this and all the rest without asking |
| `q` or `Esc` | stop here |
| `l` | replace this one, then stop ("last") |

@hints
The pattern is the whole-word one from the last lesson. The new part is
a second flag after the `g`.
--
`c` makes the substitute stop at each match, highlight it, and wait.
`y` takes it, `n` leaves it. Read each line before you answer.
--
`:%s/\<status\>/state/gc` and Enter, then answer eleven times. The
import path, the email subject, the URL and the event name get `n`;
everything else gets `y`.

@solution
    :%s/\<status\>/state/gc<Enter>  n y y y y y n n n y y  :wq<Enter>

Thirty-nine keystrokes, eleven of them decisions.

The `c` flag turns a substitute into a conversation. Each match is
highlighted in place, with the line around it, and vim waits. That is
the whole feature, and it is worth more than any of the pattern tricks
in this chapter, because it is the only one that works on meaning
rather than on text. No pattern knows that `'Your order status has
changed'` is read by a person and `order.status` is read by a
compiler. You know.

The discipline: use `c` whenever the file is longer than your screen,
whenever the word is short, whenever a string or a comment or a URL
might contain it, and whenever the replacement would be expensive to
get wrong. That is most renames. Once you have answered a few and
every remaining one looks the same, `a` takes the rest without asking.
And if you realise halfway through that the pattern is wrong, `q`
stops, `u` undoes what was done so far, and you write a better one.

Answering `n` eleven times in a row is a sign that the pattern should
have been tighter. Answering `y` to everything is a sign you could have
left the flag off. Somewhere between is where `c` earns its keep.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderStatus } from './order.status';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service';
import { EventBus } from '../../infrastructure/events/event.bus';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(EmailService) protected emailService: EmailService,
    @inject(EventBus) protected eventBus: EventBus,
  ) {}

  public async setStatus(args: { id: string; status: OrderStatus }) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id: args.id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    if (order.status === args.status) {
      return order;
    }

    const updated = await this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id: args.id },
      values: { status: args.status },
    });

    await this.emailService.send({
      to: order.customerEmail,
      subject: 'Your order status has changed',
      link: `https://shop.example.com/orders/${order.id}/status`,
    });

    this.eventBus.publish('order.status.changed', { id: order.id, status: args.status });

    return updated;
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderStatus } from './order.status';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service';
import { EventBus } from '../../infrastructure/events/event.bus';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(EmailService) protected emailService: EmailService,
    @inject(EventBus) protected eventBus: EventBus,
  ) {}

  public async setStatus(args: { id: string; state: OrderStatus }) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id: args.id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    if (order.state === args.state) {
      return order;
    }

    const updated = await this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id: args.id },
      values: { state: args.state },
    });

    await this.emailService.send({
      to: order.customerEmail,
      subject: 'Your order status has changed',
      link: `https://shop.example.com/orders/${order.id}/status`,
    });

    this.eventBus.publish('order.status.changed', { id: order.id, state: args.state });

    return updated;
  }
}
